<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dvaravati</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 1000 CE (approx.) · capital Nakhon Pathom</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Mon Buddhist civilisation of central Thailand from the 7th to 11th centuries, the earliest Theravada culture of the Chao Phraya basin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Dvaravati takes its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-pathom">Nakhon Pathom</a> and other sites inscribed 'meritorious deeds of the king of Dvaravati', matching the Duoluobodi of Chinese pilgrims. Moated towns appear in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-phraya-river">Chao Phraya</a> valley from around the 6th and 7th centuries, built by Mon-speaking communities.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Whether Dvaravati was a single kingdom or a cultural network of city-states, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-pathom">Nakhon Pathom</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramathibodi-i">U Thong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khu-bua">Khu Bua</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-thep">Si Thep</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lavo-kingdom">Lopburi</a>, is debated. Its people were early adopters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>, using Pali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-mon">Old Mon</a> in inscriptions, and produced distinctive Buddha images, stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmachakra">Wheels of the Law</a> and terracotta stupa decoration. Moats, canals and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming supported the towns.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 10th and 11th centuries the region came under the influence of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a>, and Lavo became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> centre. Dvaravati's Mon Buddhist traditions passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hariphunchai">Hariphunchai</a> in the north and fed into the later Thai kingdoms of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dvaravati.jpg" alt="Dvaravati" width="320" height="322"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Damm (User:Hdamm, Hdamm at de.wikipedia.org) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DvaravatiMapThailand.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 1000 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nakhon Pathom</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mon</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Thailand</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Thawarawadi, Duoluobodi</dd></dl></aside></div>
